---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="Behavioral Frictions and Clearer Application Boundaries" 
  description="Direct vs. indirect screen exposure, WHO/AAP guidelines, and cognitive performance boundaries for adolescents preparing for centralized exams."
>
  <article class="article-page">
    <div class="container content-width">
      <header class="article-header">
        <a href="/en/bilgi-merkezi" class="back-link">&larr; Knowledge Center</a>
        <span class="article-meta">Guide 06 / Method</span>
        <h1>Behavioral Frictions and Clearer Application Boundaries</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Frictions designed to slow down unconscious habit loops, direct/indirect exposure guidelines, and limits for exam preparation.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="article-body">
        <p>
          We check smartphones an average of 100 to 150 times a day. The vast majority of these actions are not conscious decisions, but completely automated hand habits. Picking up the phone, turning on the screen, and tapping the icon of the most used social media app is reflexively completed in seconds. What is the most effective way to break this automatic loop?
        </p>

        <h2>Behavioral Nudge and Friction Theory</h2>
        <p>
          In behavioral economics and habit psychology, any small obstacle that makes it harder to perform an action is called <strong>friction</strong>. Nudge Theory argues that instead of placing large prohibitions to change people's behavior, we can guide them to healthier choices through small changes in environment design (nudges).
        </p>
        <p>
          Adding small frictions in between your actions is very effective in breaking your automatic application-opening reflex. For example, hiding the app by deleting it from the home screen so that it can only be opened by typing in the search bar is a friction. This small obstacle prevents your finger from reflexively going there and buys enough time (cognitive control level) for your brain to ask, \"Do I really want to open this app right now?\" [6].
        </p>

        <h2>Direct and Indirect/Background Screen Exposure</h2>
        <p>
          Our relationship with devices is not limited to active use. According to the theoretical framework developed by Thompson and Tschann (2016), exposure is divided into two [65]:
        </p>
        <ul class="bullet-list" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-6); padding-left: var(--space-6); color: var(--text-secondary);">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Direct (Foreground) Exposure:</strong> Active use of the device by completely focusing the individual's attention on the content on the screen.
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
            <strong>Indirect (Background) Exposure:</strong> Even if the individual does not directly watch the device, the television or phone is turned on in the room, continuing to broadcast sound/image. Clinical studies reveal that background media reduces the quality of play for infants and young children, shortens attention spans, and delays language development [65].
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Comparative Screen Guidelines by Age Group</h2>
        <p>
          The recreational screen limits recommended by the World Health Organization (WHO), the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP), and the Ministry of Health (TR) based on child/adolescent development processes are presented comparatively below [67]:
        </p>

        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--space-6) 0; font-size: 0.875rem; text-align: left;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-color); color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 600;">
              <th style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; width: 25%;">Age Group</th>
              <th style="padding: var(--space-2);">WHO Recommendations</th>
              <th style="padding: var(--space-2);">AAP Recommendations (2026 Revision)</th>
              <th style="padding: var(--space-2);">Ministry of Health (TR)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody style="color: var(--text-secondary);">
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);">
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-primary);">0 - 18 Months</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Not recommended at all.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Screen use is prohibited, except for video chats.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Should be kept away from screens (0-3 years prohibition).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);">
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-primary);">18 - 24 Months</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Prohibited for age 1. Maximum 1 hour per day for age 2.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Only high-quality educational content with parental co-viewing.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Should be kept away from screens as much as possible.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);">
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-primary);">2 - 5 Years</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Daily maximum 1 hour.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Daily maximum 1 hour of high-quality content (co-viewing).</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Daily total time should not exceed 20-30 minutes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);">
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-primary);">6 - 12 Years</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Individual limitations.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Screen should not replace sleep and physical activity.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Daily recreational time should be at most 1 hour.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);">
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2) 0; font-weight: 500; color: var(--text-primary);">12+ Years</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Device-free bedroom and meal times.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Should be managed with a Family Media Plan; focus on digital literacy.</td>
              <td style="padding: var(--space-2);">Daily total time should be at most 120 minutes.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Limits for Adolescents Preparing for Centralized Exams</h2>
        <p>
          Child and Adolescent Psychiatrist Assoc. Prof. Ayşegül Efe emphasizes that screen time should not exceed 1 hour in primary school-age children. In particular, specific restrictions are recommended for adolescents preparing for centralized exams (LGS, YKS, etc.) to protect cognitive performance and focus [78]:
        </p>
        <p>
          For adolescents in exam preparation, <strong>limiting screen exposure to a maximum of 1 hour per day on weekdays (4 days a week) or 2 hours per day on weekends, with a weekly total of 4 hours</strong>, is critical in maintaining cognitive concentration. This restriction is one of the primary protective steps in reducing distraction and focusing difficulties, especially those triggered by short videos with rapid scene transitions (reels, shorts, etc.) [78].
        </p>

        <h2>Limitra: Your Digital Boundary Assistant</h2>
        <p>
          Whether you want to protect your children's study focus or manage your own digital habits, using clear barriers instead of flexible boundaries increases the success rate. <strong>Limitra</strong> clearly blocks the relevant application until the end of the day when your set time limit expires.
        </p>
        <p>
          This way, you can ensure that your children preparing for exams stay loyal to their daily 1-hour limits, as recommended by Assoc. Prof. Ayşegül Efe, and prevent insidious time losses.
        </p>

        <div class="info-box" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <div class="info-box-title">Protect Your Time with Clearer Boundaries</div>
          <p>
            Limitra is a simple digital discipline tool designed for people who want to reduce social media usage, increase focus, and stay loyal to their rational decisions.
          </p>
          <div style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <a href="/en/limitra" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm">Discover Limitra</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

